---
import CourseLesson from "../../../layouts/CourseLesson.astro";
import Math from "../../../components/course/Math.astro";
import Callout from "../../../components/course/Callout.astro";
import Quiz from "../../../components/course/Quiz.astro";
import ReliabilityNetwork from "../../../components/course/widgets/ReliabilityNetwork.astro";
---

<CourseLesson course="probability" lessonSlug="reliability-networks">
  <h2>What a block diagram means</h2>
  <p>
    A reliability network is a picture of an event, not a circuit. Each block is a component that
    works with probability <Math tex="p_i" />, independently of the others. The system works when a
    connected path of working blocks runs from the input to the output.
  </p>
  <p>Two patterns generate everything else.</p>

  <h3>Series: every block must work</h3>
  <p>
    A path through <Math tex="n" /> blocks in a row needs all of them, and independence turns the
    intersection into a product:
  </p>
  <Math display tex="p_{\text{series}} = p_1 p_2 \cdots p_n" />
  <p>
    Since each factor is at most 1, a chain is never more reliable than its weakest link &mdash; it
    is strictly worse than every one of them.
  </p>

  <h3>Parallel: at least one must work</h3>
  <p>
    "At least one" is the complement of "none", exactly as in the axioms lesson. Every block must
    fail for a parallel bank to fail, and failures are independent, so:
  </p>
  <Math display tex="p_{\text{parallel}} = 1 - (1-p_1)(1-p_2)\cdots(1-p_n)" />
  <p>
    Redundancy compounds: three mediocre 0.6 blocks in parallel give{" "}
    <Math tex="1 - 0.4^3 = 0.936" />, better than any single one of them.
  </p>

  <Callout variant="intuition" label="Work in failure probabilities">
    <p>
      Write <Math tex="q_i = 1 - p_i" />. Then series multiplies the <Math tex="p" />'s and parallel
      multiplies the <Math tex="q" />'s &mdash; the two rules are mirror images. Switching to
      whichever number is small keeps the arithmetic accurate and makes the structure obvious.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Reducing a network</h2>
  <p>
    The method is mechanical: find any sub-block that is purely series or purely parallel, replace it
    with a single equivalent block, and repeat until one block remains. Take the network in the
    widget below &mdash; a three-branch parallel bank, one lone block, then a two-branch parallel
    bank.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      The bottom-left branch is two blocks in series:{" "}
      <Math tex="0.9 \times 0.2 = 0.18" />. Notice how badly the 0.2 drags down the 0.9.
    </li>
    <li>
      That branch now sits in parallel with 0.8 and 0.6:{" "}
      <Math tex="1 - (0.2)(0.4)(0.82) = 1 - 0.0656 = 0.9344" />.
    </li>
    <li>The upper-right branch is series: <Math tex="0.6 \times 0.5 = 0.30" />.</li>
    <li>
      In parallel with the 0.4 branch:{" "}
      <Math tex="1 - (0.70)(0.60) = 1 - 0.42 = 0.58" />.
    </li>
    <li>
      The three survivors are in series:{" "}
      <Math tex="0.9344 \times 0.40 \times 0.58 \approx 0.2168" />.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <Math display tex="p_{\text{system}} \approx 0.2168" />
  <p>
    The lesson is in the shape of the answer: a strong redundant bank (0.9344) is thrown away by a
    single unbacked 0.4 block sitting in series with it. In a series chain, the cheapest reliability
    improvement is always the worst block.
  </p>

  <ReliabilityNetwork />

  <Callout variant="warning" label="Not every network reduces this way">
    <p>
      Series&ndash;parallel reduction only works when the diagram really is nested series and
      parallel pieces. A bridge network &mdash; where a cross-link joins two otherwise separate
      branches &mdash; has no such decomposition, and you must condition on the bridge component:
      compute the system reliability once assuming it works and once assuming it fails, then average
      with the law of total probability. That fallback always works.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <Quiz
    question="Two components with p = 0.9 each. How much more reliable is putting them in parallel rather than in series?"
    options={[
      "0.81 vs 0.99 — parallel is better by 0.18",
      "0.99 vs 0.81 — parallel is better by 0.18",
      "They are the same",
      "0.90 vs 0.81 — parallel is better by 0.09",
    ]}
    correctIndex={1}
    correctText="Correct: series gives 0.9 × 0.9 = 0.81; parallel gives 1 − 0.1 × 0.1 = 0.99. Redundancy squares the failure probability instead of the success probability."
    incorrectText="Series multiplies successes: 0.9 × 0.9 = 0.81. Parallel multiplies failures: 1 − (0.1)(0.1) = 0.99. Parallel wins by 0.18."
  />
</CourseLesson>
